  (previously 72px / three lines).

Note for anyone rebuilding: do not run `next build` while `next dev` is running.
The build rewrites .next and the dev server then throws React Client Manifest /
__webpack_modules__ errors. Stop dev, `rm -rf .next`, then build.

================================================================================
[1.0.2] 2026-09-21 — FIX — Reveal fail-safe made deterministic and correct
================================================================================
Two problems with the [0.9.0] fail-safe, both found by instrumenting the live
page rather than trusting it:

1. It did not actually work. Handing an already-mounted motion element a new
   `animate` prop does not reliably pull it off a half-applied transform. The
   primitives now branch on element TYPE instead: when the reveal cannot run
   they render plain <div>/<h1>/<span> rather than motion components, which
   forces a clean remount with no leftover styles. PlanCard and FiberPath keep
   their motion elements (they need hover tilt / SVG variants) and swap `key`
   to force the same remount. TextReveal's static path keeps the gradient
   accent, so the headline still reads the same, just without the reveal.
   Measured with the fallback forced on: 123 hidden elements → 0.

2. It fired for the wrong reason. Diagnosis of the preview tab: document.hidden
   was true and requestAnimationFrame delivered 0 callbacks per second — even
   the pure-CSS marquee sat frozen at currentTime 0. Browsers suspend rAF in a
   background tab, and IntersectionObserver rides the same frame pipeline, so a
   backgrounded tab is indistinguishable from a broken one by observation. The
   original probe would have concluded "broken" and permanently disabled the
   animations for anyone who opened the page in a background tab. The probe now
   waits for visibilityState === 'visible' before it starts, and re-checks
   visibility before concluding, retrying on the next visible moment. Nothing
   is lost by waiting: while the tab is hidden, nobody is looking at it.

Also moved the hero's promo pills and eyebrow off bare mount animations and
onto the guarded Reveal primitive — they were the last two pieces of
above-the-fold content not covered by the fallback.

Verification note: the reveals were confirmed animating normally earlier in the
session while the preview pane was foregrounded. The frozen-frame behaviour
above is the browser doing the right thing for a hidden tab, not a page defect.

