================================================================================
[2.0.1] 2026-09-21 — FIX — Root cause of the invisible headings
================================================================================
Every H2 on the site was invisible in a healthy browser, and the reason was not
the background-tab freezing diagnosed in [1.0.2].

TextReveal wrapped each word in an `overflow-hidden` span and parked it at
translateY(108%) until it scrolled into view. IntersectionObserver clips a
target's intersection rect against its ancestors' overflow — so a word sitting
entirely below its own clipping parent reports isIntersecting:false forever. The
reveal could never fire. It is self-defeating by construction: the element hid
itself in the one place the observer watching it cannot see.

That is why the symptom was so selective — eyebrows and paragraphs used opacity
and stayed in the observer's view, so they revealed normally; only the clipped
headings vanished.

Fix: headings no longer use a scroll-triggered transform at all. `Heading`
renders plain markup with a CSS `rise` keyframe. Two deliberate choices:
  • transform-only, NO opacity — a heading must be legible at every frame,
    including frame zero, which is where a background tab freezes it. A 14px
    offset costs nothing; an invisible headline costs the page.
  • `animation-fill-mode: forwards`, not `both` — with `forwards` the element's
    default style is its visible state, so a heading whose animation never
    starts still renders. `both` would back-fill the hidden start frame.
Verified: all 10 page headings report opacity 1 in a frozen background tab.

Also fixed while verifying the rebuilt cards:
- The "Most popular" badge sat beside the plan name and stole enough width to
  wrap that one card's title onto two lines, knocking the row of titles out of
  alignment. The badge now has its own row, reserved on every card.
- "600 Mbps down · 600 Mbps up" was the one speed string long enough to wrap at
  a quarter-width card. Every tier is symmetrical, so it now reads
  "600 Mbps symmetrical" — same fact, one line.
- Verified across all four plan grids: every title one line, every card in a row
  the same height.

