[2.2.0] 2026-09-22 — STYLE — Hero rebuilt; copy raised to a professional register
================================================================================
Client review: the hero read as generic, the promotion was "an old generic box",
the headline lacked emphasis, several section titles were too conversational,
Why Hawaiian Telcom felt generic, and the page wanted vibrant animated colour.

HERO — rebuilt
- The full-bleed background + scrim approach is gone. It produced a muddy middle
  ground where the photograph was neither a clean backdrop nor a proper feature,
  and the offer panel covered whatever was left. The photograph is now contained
  in a shaped rounded frame with its own composition.
- The promotion is no longer a boxed card. It renders as a tag clipped to the
  photograph's lower-left corner, carrying an animated brand-gradient rule down
  its leading edge, a live pulse indicator, the rate set large, and a sheen that
  passes across on a loop. On small screens it drops into normal flow beneath the
  frame.
- Headline emphasis: a marker-style highlight wipes in behind the rate line,
  which is the single most important phrase in the hero.
- Added an eyebrow rule, and a floating "3 Gbps / up & down" chip on the frame.
- Entrance choreography via framer-motion: eyebrow, headline lines, subhead,
  trust marks and ZIP field enter on a staggered spring. Deliberately
  MOUNT-driven, not scroll-driven — nothing above the fold may wait on an
  observer, which is what stranded the old headings.

GradientField.tsx — new
- Vibrant animated colour for section backgrounds: three blurred radial blobs
  drifting on transform only. No canvas, no JavaScript, nothing that touches
  layout. Colours are the exact scraped hexes (#4294F7, #22D3C5, #3DC37F) at low
  alpha over white, so the page stays bright rather than tinted.
- `bold` in the hero, `soft` behind fiber, bundles, TV, phone, fine print and
  FAQ. Decorative, so aria-hidden and pointer-events-none throughout.
- prefers-reduced-motion freezes it to a static wash via the global rule.

WHY HAWAIIAN TELCOM — refined
- Added a navy metric band leading with the three figures that matter (3 Gbps,
  99.99%, 1883) over an internal brand-gradient wash, each entering on a stagger.
- Feature cards reworked: tinted icon chip that fills with brand blue on hover,
  and a gradient rule that draws across the top edge. The flat white cards with a
  coloured number were the generic part.

COPY — professional register
  "Symmetrical fiber, all the way to the house."
    -> "Fiber optic service delivered directly to the residence."
  "Bundle it, and the rate drops."
    -> "Combine services and reduce your monthly rate."
  "Live TV and your streaming apps, one guide."
    -> "Live television and streaming, unified in one guide."
  "A landline that still makes sense."
    -> "Home voice service built for reliability."
  "A line that works when everything else stops."
    -> "Service that remains available during an outage."
  "What is included, and what costs extra."
    -> "Complete disclosure of inclusions and applicable fees."
  "A fiber network built for these islands."
    -> "Infrastructure engineered for the Hawaiian Islands."
  "Fiber run the whole way to the house."
    -> "Fiber terminated at your property."
  "One account, one bill, one install."
    -> "A single account, one bill, one installation."
  "Your channels and your apps in one place."
    -> "Channels and applications in a single interface."
  "Street by street, across the islands."
    -> "Expanding across the islands, address by address."
  Supporting body copy under each was raised to match, and every `accentFrom`
  index was re-pointed so the blue phrase still falls on the right words.
- "1883 / Serving Hawaiʻi since" read backwards as a value-and-label pair;
  the label is now "Established in Hawaiʻi".

Verified: build clean, 12/12 static. Headings all report opacity 1. No
horizontal overflow at 375px, where the offer tag returns to normal flow.

================================================================================
[2.3.0] 2026-09-22 — ANIMATION — Hero motion deepened; colour field per section
================================================================================
HERO — additional motion
- Word-by-word headline reveal, each word rising and rotating on its X axis from
  a bottom origin. Deliberately NOT clipped by an overflow-hidden parent: that
  construction hides the element outside the very box an observer measures, and
  is what left every heading on this site invisible once before. These animate on
  mount, on transform and opacity only.
- Animated gradient sweeping through the rate line, clipped to the glyphs and
  travelling continuously (#4294F7 → #22D3C5 → #3DC37F → #7D61B3).
- Cursor-tracking spotlight across the whole hero: a soft blue/teal radial that
  follows the pointer on a spring. Skipped for reduced motion and coarse
  pointers, so it never runs on touch.
- Rotating conic halo around the photo frame, in the four brand colours.
- Slow scale drift on the photograph — a restrained Ken Burns over 24s.
- Scroll-linked parallax: the photo frame drifts as the hero scrolls away.
- Second floating statistic ("99.99% reliability") joining the 3 Gbps chip, each
  on its own float cycle, both now frosted with a backdrop blur.
- Scroll cue at the foot of the section.

GRADIENT FIELD — richer and per-section
- Added a fourth layer: a wide aurora ribbon sweeping across the middle band.
- Raised blob opacity (soft 0.28→0.40, bold 0.50→0.60).
- Added a `variant` prop so each section gets its own colour mix, and the page
  moves through a shifting field rather than repeating one wash six times:
    hero blue · fiber blue · bundles teal · TV violet · phone green
    fine print sunrise · why teal · FAQ violet
  Sunrise is the only variant using #FC8A52 and #F6D051, both scraped hexes.
- Still transform/opacity only, no canvas, no JavaScript.

FIXES FOUND WHILE BUILDING THIS
1. The rotating conic halo rendered as a giant diamond behind the photograph.
   Spinning a rounded rectangle sweeps its corners far outside its own box. The
   rotating layer now lives inside a clipping wrapper and is oversized (160%),
   so only the glow shows.
2. The accent headline went completely invisible. `background-clip: text` was on
   the wrapper while each word carried its own transform — a transformed child
   establishes its own painting context, so the parent's clip cannot reach the
   child's glyphs, yet `color: transparent` still inherits down. The gradient now
   sits on each word span. Verified in the DOM: every word reports both a
   gradient background-image and `background-clip: text`.
3. `.text-gradient-sweep` paints solid #4294F7 by default and only becomes
   transparent inside an `@supports` guard, so a browser that cannot clip a
   background to text still renders a readable headline.
4. The reliability chip sat over the subject's face; moved clear.

Note: one build failed with a next/font error fetching Google Fonts
("Cannot read properties of null"). Transient network failure at build time,
unrelated to any change here — the identical build succeeded on retry.

Verified: build clean, 12/12 static, 176 kB First Load JS. All 8 page headings
carry rendered text. Scroll reveals confirmed transitioning 0 → 1 on entry, with
nothing left hidden once a section is in view.

================================================================================
[2.3.1] 2026-09-22 — FIX — Headline legibility and the photo frame treatment
================================================================================
1. ROTATING CONIC HALO REMOVED.
   Even clipped, the spinning border threw hard-edged teal wedges past the
   photograph's corners and read as a gimmick rather than a finish. Replaced
   with a still, blurred brand glow bedded under the frame — quieter, more
   expensive-looking, and cheaper to composite. Verified on the live page: zero
   conic layers and zero spinning elements remain in the hero.

2. HEADLINE GRADIENT WAS AN ACCESSIBILITY FAILURE, NOT JUST A PALE ONE.
   The rate line ran through #22D3C5 and #3DC37F, measured against white:
       #4294F7  3.08:1  pass        #22D3C5  1.88:1  FAIL
       #7D61B3  4.96:1  pass        #3DC37F  2.26:1  FAIL
   WCAG requires 3:1 for large text, so roughly half of "at just $30/mo." was
   below the readability floor — which is exactly how it looked on screen.
   The gradient is now blues only, every stop clearing the threshold:
       #4294F7 3.08:1 → #005BB5 6.64:1 → #072B6F 13.26:1 → #005BB5 → #4294F7
   Teal and green remain in the background colour fields, where they sit behind
   nothing that has to be read.

3. MARKER BAND strengthened — held at full strength to 68% of its length rather
   than fading immediately, so the whole rate line sits on even backing instead
   of trailing off into the colour field.

The `spin-slow` keyframe is now unreferenced; Tailwind emits keyframes only for
animation utilities that appear in the source, so no dead CSS ships.

================================================================================
[2.3.2] 2026-09-22 — FIX — Fonts self-hosted; build no longer needs the network
================================================================================
The next/font Google Fonts failure logged as "transient" in [2.3.0] recurred, so
it is not transient — it is a standing fragility. `next/font/google` fetches the
stylesheet and the font binaries from Google at BUILD time, so any network blip
fails the build outright:

    An error occurred in `next/font`.
    TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')

Confirmed independently: a plain Node `fetch()` to fonts.googleapis.com timed out
from this machine while `curl` to the same host succeeded, which is exactly the
intermittent reachability that breaks the build. Locally this is an annoyance;
in CI it is a red pipeline on a commit that changed nothing.

FIX — both faces are now self-hosted via next/font/local:
    src/app/fonts/PlusJakartaSans-latin.woff2      26.7 KB
    src/app/fonts/PlusJakartaSans-latin-ext.woff2  21.2 KB
    src/app/fonts/Inter-latin.woff2                47.1 KB
    src/app/fonts/Inter-latin-ext.woff2            83.1 KB
- Variable weight axes, so one file per subset covers every weight in use.
- latin-ext is included because it carries the macron in Hawaiian place names.
- Explicit `fallback` stacks so a failed font load degrades to Helvetica/Arial
  rather than an unstyled default.

Secondary benefits:
- Builds are now reproducible and network-independent, byte-identical each run.
- Drops the runtime connection to fonts.gstatic.com — one fewer third party
  receiving visitor IP addresses, which matters for the privacy policy this site
  publishes.

Verified on the live page: document.fonts reports both families loaded, the h1
resolves to the display face, and performance.getEntriesByType('resource')
returns ZERO requests to googleapis or gstatic.

================================================================================
[2.4.0] 2026-09-22 — COMPONENT — Shared icon set; icons added to cards
================================================================================
Audit first: PlanCard carried only a feature checkmark, FinePrintGrid had no
icons at all, and WhyUs kept a private icon set of its own — which is how two
icon styles end up drifting apart on the same page.

NEW — src/components/Icons.tsx, the site's single icon set.
- 16 glyphs on one 24x24 grid at 1.7 stroke, round caps and joins, all inheriting
  currentColor so an icon takes its container's colour with no fill override.
    service lines  fiber · bundle · tv · phone
    feature grid   shield · sync · price · island · wifi
    inclusions     data · activation · equipment · billing · term · speed ·
                   availability
- Every icon is decorative and sits beside text that already carries the meaning,
  so all are aria-hidden with focusable="false" rather than labelled — a label
  here would simply be announced twice.

APPLIED
- PlanCard: a service-line badge in the card header, keyed off `plan.serviceLine`.
  Tinted by default, filling with brand blue on hover, and solid blue on the
  popular card. The badge shares the row previously reserved for "Most popular",
  so titles stay aligned across the grid.
- FinePrintGrid: an icon per row in both presentations — inline beside the row
  header in the desktop table, and in a tinted chip in the mobile cards.
- WhyUs: private icon component deleted; now imports the shared set.

DATA
- `FinePrintRow` gained a typed `icon` field and all seven rows were populated,
  so the icon choice lives in content.ts beside the copy it labels rather than
  as a lookup table buried in a component. Adding a row now requires an icon,
  enforced by the type.

Verified on the live page: 4/4 plan cards carry a line icon, 7/7 table rows and
7/7 mobile cards carry a row icon, 6/6 feature cards render, and zero icons are
missing aria-hidden.

================================================================================
[3.0.0] 2026-09-22 — BUILD — Static HTML export
================================================================================
The site now builds to plain HTML. `npm run build` emits an `out/` folder of
real .html files plus the CSS, JS, fonts and images they reference, deployable
to any static host with no Node runtime behind it.

APPROACH — Next's static export, not a hand-written rewrite.
The requirement was that everything stay the same, so the safest path was to
pre-render the existing code rather than reimplement it in vanilla HTML/CSS/JS.
A hand rewrite would have meant rebuilding the framer-motion choreography by
hand and would inevitably have drifted from the original. The export ships the
identical markup, styles and scripts — only the delivery changes.

PRE-FLIGHT — verified nothing blocked export:
  no API routes · no middleware · no server actions
  /legal/[slug] already supplies generateStaticParams

CONFIG — next.config.mjs
  output: 'export'          emit static HTML
  trailingSlash: true       /legal/privacy/index.html, so hosts resolve the URL
                            without rewrite rules
  images: { unoptimized: true }
                            the image optimizer is a server feature. No loss
                            here: the set was already re-encoded to WebP at
                            0.71 MB, and blur placeholders survive because they
                            are inlined as base64 at build time.

OUTPUT — 11 HTML files, 3.5 MB total
  index.html 171 KB · css 32 KB · js 1.04 MB · fonts 178 KB
  All 10 routes verified serving 200 from plain files.

VERIFIED IN THE EXPORT — nothing lost
  ✓ 13 CSS animations live: drift-a, drift-b, ribbon, marker, drift-scale,
    float-y, sweep, shimmer, pulse-ring, cue, marquee, rise (+ framer-motion)
  ✓ Headline gradient stops intact; 24 gradient-field layers; 94 inline icons;
    6 images with blur placeholders
  ✓ React hydrates: ZIP validation, FAQ accordion and mobile nav all toggle
  ✓ ZERO external requests — the self-hosted fonts from [2.3.2] mean the page
    contacts nothing but its own origin
  ✓ index.html is genuine pre-rendered markup (plan names, prices and copy are
    in the HTML), not a JavaScript shell — so it indexes without execution

NOTED
- out/images/ carries plain-named copies of the six section photographs that
  nothing requests; pages use content-hashed copies under _next/static/media for
  caching. Harmless — they are never fetched — but they can be pruned from an
  upload. og-share.jpg must stay, as the social card metadata points at it.
- Serve over HTTP, not file://: assets are root-relative (/_next/...).
- Subfolder hosting needs basePath + assetPrefix set before building.

Added `npm run preview` to serve out/ locally, and a deployment section in
README.md.

================================================================================
[3.1.0] 2026-09-22 — RESPONSIVE — Mobile pass, then published to GitHub
================================================================================
AUDIT — measured at 320, 375, 768, 1024 and 1440 px.
Layout and type were already sound: no horizontal overflow at any width, grids
collapsing correctly, nav folding to a sheet below 1280, the fine-print table
swapping to cards below 1024, h1 at 40px and body at 18px on a 320px screen, and
the only sub-40px tap target being the sr-only skip link.

The problem was the animation budget, not the layout.

On a 320x720 viewport (0.23 megapixels) the page was compositing 33 blurred
layers covering 12.1 MEGAPIXELS of blurred surface — roughly fifty times the
screen area, repainting every frame — plus 59 elements carrying `will-change`,
each pinning a layer in GPU memory. A large blur is among the most expensive
things a browser can composite and the cost scales with area, so on a mid-range
phone this means dropped frames, a warm device and a flat battery.

FIXES
- GradientField now runs at half strength below `sm`: two blobs instead of four
  (the third blob and the aurora ribbon are `hidden sm:block`), blur radius
  52px instead of 110px, and tighter sizes. Layers return at `sm` and above, so
  desktop is visually unchanged.
- Removed `will-change` from the gradient blobs, the hero photograph, the conic
  layer and the parallax wrapper: all are already transform-animated, so the
  browser promotes them anyway and the hint only pinned extra layers.
- Scoped the remaining two `will-change` hints to `lg`. They sit on the magnetic
  button and the 3D tilt card, both of which are skipped on coarse pointers —
  so a phone was paying for effects it never receives.
- Hero photograph min height 20rem -> 17rem, so the frame does not dominate a
  small screen before any copy is reached.
- Header wordmark held on one line and the "Authorized Retailer" sub-line hidden
  below `sm`. At 320px it broke across two rows and stopped reading as a mark;
  the disclosure bar directly above states the relationship in full anyway.

RESULT at 320px:  blurred surface 12.1 MP -> 1.9 MP (84% less)
                  will-change layers 59 -> 0
                  running animations 45 -> 29
Desktop at 1440px re-verified unchanged: full four-layer fields, nav visible,
no overflow.

PUBLISHED
- git init, single commit, pushed to
  https://github.com/PavithraEbbali/hawaiiantelcom (branch main).
- 45 files. node_modules, .next and out/ excluded; the self-hosted fonts and the
  seven images are committed, since the build needs them.
- Removed a leftover one-off font-download script before committing.

VERCEL READINESS
- `npm ci --dry-run` passes, so the lockfile is in sync with package.json —
  the usual cause of a Vercel build failing when local builds work.
- Added engines.node >=18.18.0 so a future change to Vercel's default Node
  cannot silently break the build.
- No vercel.json needed: Vercel detects Next.js, runs the build and serves the
  exported out/ folder. No environment variables. The build touches no network,
  because the fonts are committed.

================================================================================
[3.2.0] 2026-09-22 — BUILD — Export slimmed; site packaged as a zip
================================================================================
Packaging the static site for handoff surfaced two problems in the export.

1. INTERNAL DOCS WERE BEING PUBLISHED.
   public/images/IMAGE-SPEC.md — notes on the image set, including the
   AI-watermark caveat and the placeholder phone number — was copied into the
   build and would have been readable at yourdomain.com/images/IMAGE-SPEC.md.
   Everything in public/ is served verbatim; documentation does not belong there.

2. EVERY SECTION PHOTOGRAPH SHIPPED TWICE.
   The six photographs sat in public/, which Next copies wholesale, AND were
   statically imported, which emits a separate content-hashed copy. The pages
   only ever load the hashed copies — verified against the built HTML, which
   references /_next/static/media/*.webp plus /images/og-share.jpg and nothing
   else — so the plain-named set was 610 KB of dead weight in every build.

FIX — moved the imported images out of the served directory:
    src/assets/images/   the six section photographs (imported only)
    public/images/       og-share.jpg alone, which needs a fixed URL because
                         social scrapers fetch it by address from the metadata
    IMAGES.md            the notes, now at the repo root and not served
Import paths updated in Hero, WhyUs and page.tsx to use the @/assets alias.

RESULT: export 59 files / 3.35 MB -> 52 files / 2.74 MB.
Verified after the move: every image request returns 200, all headings visible,
animations running, hero renders unchanged.

ZIP — hawaiian-telcom-site.zip
- 53 files, 2.68 MB uncompressed, 1.35 MB compressed. Integrity checked.
- Archive paths are relative to out/, so index.html sits at the ROOT of the zip.
  Hosts expect that on upload; a wrapping folder is the usual cause of a
  "site uploaded but shows a directory listing" support call.
- Includes READ-ME-FIRST.txt written for a non-developer: where to upload, the
  warning not to open index.html off disk (assets are root-relative), the
  subfolder caveat, and the three placeholders still to replace before launch.
- *.zip added to .gitignore — it is a build artefact, reproducible from source.
